INT. HOTEL ROOM - GRAND PACIFIC - NIGHT, 1951

Art deco lamp. A rain-streaked window. A wire recorder on the writing desk, its spools motionless.

VIVIENNE LAROCHE-STANTON, 30s, evening gown, unpinned hair, sits on the edge of the bed. She stares at the recorder as though it might speak first.

She stands. Crosses. Presses PLAY.

VIVIENNE (V.O.)
(recorded, bright)
Darling, it's me. If you're hearing this, I've made it to San Francisco and the hotel has the most dreadful wallpaper.

Vivienne smiles at the wallpaper. It is dreadful.

VIVIENNE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I'll see you at the Cliff House at nine. Wear the blue tie.

She stops it. Crosses to the window. Checks her wristwatch. 11:40.

VIVIENNE
(quiet)
Nine.

She returns. Rewinds. Plays again.

VIVIENNE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recorded, bright)
Darling, it's me.

She snaps it off. Paces. Picks up the telephone receiver. Puts it down.

She opens the closet. A blue tie hangs there, tags still on. She pulls it free. Holds it.

VIVIENNE (CONT'D)
You said you'd come.

She wraps the tie around her fist.

VIVIENNE (CONT'D)
You said you'd come, and you didn't, and I wore the dress.

She flings the tie across the room. It slaps the wall. She picks up the recorder, lifts it above her head, and hurls it at the wardrobe mirror.

CRASH. The mirror shatters. The spools unspool in long brown ribbons.

Silence. Rain. She stands among the shards, breathing.

Then, from the broken machine, a last crackle of voice.

VIVIENNE (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recorded)
...and darling, whatever happens, I'll wait.

Vivienne sinks to the floor. The lamp flickers.
